WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:23.080
Demand for housing in Washington DC is going
through the roof.

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:27.860
Over a thousand people move into the city
every month, driving up the cost of housing

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:32.220
and they're turning the nation's capital into
a construction zone.

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:37.540
Tower cranes piercing the sky above the city
streets have become so common

00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:39.640
they're just part of the background.

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:43.620
But as fast as the cranes have gone up,

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:46.680
demand for housing is rising even faster.

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:51.140
Making DC among the most expensive places
to live in the United States.

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:53.180
And one innovation whose time has come

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:57.020
shows just why the demand for housing is far
from being met.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:08.400
I kinda got driven down the tiny house road
because of affordability,

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:10.720
simplicity, sustainability and then mobility.

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:14.520
Tiny houses are very cheap to build, you can
build one of these for ten thousand dollars

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:17.000
you can build one for thirty, forty, fifty
thousand dollars,

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:21.700
but they definitely come in far beneath the
cost of a regular home

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:24.180
especially in a city like DC.

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:28.340
They're very sustainable, they take very little
energy to heat or cool.

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:31.260
So having a space that I could hook up to
solar

00:01:31.270 --> 00:01:35.960
that I could, you know, catch rain water and
use for a simple shower and sink

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:37.820
was very very appealing to me.

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:41.080
They are mobile because many of them are built
on wheels

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:43.939
but the reason they're often built on wheels
is because it then escapes

00:01:43.939 --> 00:01:50.200
a lot of the kind of coding requirements which
these homes would violate

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:52.200
not because they're unsafe places to live,

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:57.939
but because the minimum size of a room you
know must be 120 square feet

00:01:57.940 --> 00:02:04.040
and if these homes are 120 square feet altogether
you then have obvious issues.

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:08.660
This is not a tiny house, it's the Office
of Zoning, the Zoning Comission,

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:12.260
the Zoning Administrator, the Board of Zoning
Adjustment and the Office of Planning

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:15.430
which, along with the Department of Consumer
and Regulatory Affairs

00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:21.400
implements, adjudicates and enforces 34 chapters
and 600 pages of regulations

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:25.680
governing property use and building requirements
int he nation's capital.

00:02:26.380 --> 00:02:29.200
The director of the city's Office of Planning,
Ellen McCarthy,

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:32.640
described the problem with allowing tiny houses
in the city:

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:47.240
and so the zoning authorities have allowed
a few experiments in affordable housing

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:51.420
like this apartment made entirely from shipping
containers.

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:57.380
But the city is constrained by a zoning ordinance
that was drawn up in 1958

00:02:57.380 --> 00:03:02.020
that's 56 years of cultural change and building
innovations, like tiny houses,

00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:05.580
that the code wasn't designed to address.

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:10.340
and for small builders like Jay Austin, getting
a special exemption from the zoning authorities

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:14.020
could cost tens of thousands of dollars and
years of time

00:03:14.020 --> 00:03:16.420
with no guarantee of success.

00:03:16.430 --> 00:03:19.680
The proposed changes to zoning rewrite won't
effect what we're doing at all,

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:23.560
we are, tiny homes on wheels are still a little
bit further out

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:25.360
probably many years out, of decades out.

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:29.660
It's totally legal to buy one of these and
to park it somewhere.

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:37.180
And really it only becomes illegal once you
step inside and say 'this is my home.'

00:03:37.180 --> 00:03:41.220
We could leave these houses on the lot I imagine
for 20 years

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:42.960
and there wouldn't be any issue with it

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:45.980
but if we declared these are full time residences
then there, you know,

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:50.760
would be a little bit more, little bit more
trouble perhaps.

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:53.500
The laws that keep Jay Austin from living
in his own home

00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:58.140
have their genesis in New York City's zoning
resolution of 1916.

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:00.820
For the first time in history, committees
of urban planners

00:04:00.820 --> 00:04:04.580
began to reshape an entire American city.

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:09.420
They split up skyscrapers to provide more
sunlight on the streets below.

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:15.580
Industrial factories were separated from residential
property.

00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:19.120
Immigrant communities were kept apart from
neighborhoods of the elite.

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:23.500
And when the supreme court ruled these laws
didn't violate property rights,

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:27.980
zoning quickly spread to every major city
in America.

00:04:27.980 --> 00:04:31.480
Every city, that is, except for one.

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:03.200
Houston for me is an experiment.

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:08.520
It's an experiment in overt capitalism.

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:11.400
And therefore very much at home in Texas,

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:15.800
there is a kind of libertarian way of thinking here.

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:21.939
It's much more alive in a way, much more organic,
than a typical zoned city

00:05:21.939 --> 00:05:26.830
that is more restricted by these old fashioned
measures that no longer are viable.

00:05:26.830 --> 00:05:31.860
If you live in an unzoned city, you have to
develop a dialogue or negotiation.

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:36.319
And that of course we have seen in our country
is not easy to come by.

00:05:36.319 --> 00:05:40.409
I have architect friends that have to operate
in places like Berkeley

00:05:40.409 --> 00:05:48.939
where the zoning board is sort of a group
of aestheticians without training

00:05:48.939 --> 00:05:52.610
say 'that's no good, this is good, this is
Berkeley, this is not.'

00:05:52.610 --> 00:05:59.279
And this sort of, you know, it's silliness
but has enormous economic consequences

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:02.200
is operating in those places.

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:05.720
While here, you can almost get away with anything.

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:13.160
Anything, like a house clad entirely in beer
cans.

00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:17.700
In Houston a few simple laws govern lot sizes
and set backs from the street.

00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:21.100
There's even a new historical preservation
ordinance.

00:06:21.100 --> 00:06:24.620
But for the most part, developments are regulated
by private covenants

00:06:24.620 --> 00:06:26.600
and deed restrictions.

00:06:26.600 --> 00:06:30.240
And without city codes, committees or planners
to regulate land use,

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:35.460
all sorts of creative expression are possible.

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:38.439
When architects began to build homes out of
corrugated metal,

00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:41.840
a cheap material associated with poverty and
trailer parks,

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:44.080
no one had the authority to stop them.

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:48.200
As it turned out not only were tin houses
economical,

00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:54.380
they also kept homes cool by reflecting sunlight
during Houston's sweltering summers.

00:06:54.380 --> 00:06:57.919
Today tin houses are cherished as a unique
Houston innovation.

00:06:59.720 --> 00:07:02.780
Whether it's a tin house in Houston or a McMansion
in McAllen,

00:07:02.780 --> 00:07:04.500
homes in the sprawling state of Texas

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:08.500
will always be cheaper than the densely populated
northeast.

00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:12.760
But compared to the rest of the sun belt where
cities are zoned and the land is plentiful,

00:07:12.760 --> 00:07:18.520
unregulated Houston is still the most affordable
large city in America.

00:07:18.520 --> 00:07:22.580
I totally agree that regulation is about preventing
bad things from happening.

00:07:22.580 --> 00:07:25.860
Houston's success in creating affordable housing
for the middle class

00:07:25.860 --> 00:07:31.160
is one reason why the city is disparaged by
the one group whose job it makes obsolete.

00:07:31.160 --> 00:07:36.420
Urban planners like Harriet Tregoning, formerly
of the Washington DC Office of Planning.

00:07:36.420 --> 00:07:38.580
I hate to make Houston the whipping boy, right?

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:40.320
Oh go ahead.

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:43.930
You know, that's a place that doesn't have
zoning, it doesn't' have regulation

00:07:43.930 --> 00:07:48.960
and it's not exactly the full flower of urbanism.

00:07:54.280 --> 00:07:57.460
Two hours down the gulf coast from Houston
is Victoria.

00:07:57.460 --> 00:08:02.720
A city that hasn't had zoning since it was
founded 190 years ago.

00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:09.120
And the most unusual thing about Victoria
is that it's not very unusual at all.

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:13.520
I don't notice anything greatly different
about Victoria

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:16.400
and the look that Victoria portrays

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:19.720
and the look that they have in San Antonio,
Austin or Houston.

00:08:19.720 --> 00:08:26.960
So, property rights in Texas are sacred, you
can do what you want to do with your property

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:32.340
and I think most of the people here in Victoria
want to protect that right.

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:38.260
The city's growth and land use is based more
on economics and the will of the owner

00:08:38.260 --> 00:08:43.280
rather than some quasi-governmental body that
you have to ask permission from.

00:08:43.280 --> 00:08:48.630
East of town was a rendering plant where they
took dead animals and processed them.

00:08:48.630 --> 00:08:51.520
The people that were in favor of zoning would
say

00:08:51.520 --> 00:08:54.460
'well what if there's a rendering plant next
to your house?'

00:08:54.460 --> 00:08:59.500
Economics dictate that you're not going to
put a rendering plant

00:08:59.500 --> 00:09:02.240
next to a residential subdivision

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:07.380
economics dictates that you're gonna build
a shopping center on a major thoroughfare.

00:09:07.380 --> 00:09:14.240
It's worked very well in Victoria for a number
of years and I trust we'll continue to do so.

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:17.420
Now it's true if you look hard enough in Victoria
or Houston

00:09:17.420 --> 00:09:21.560
you can find the odd high rise poking out
of a low density neighborhood

00:09:21.560 --> 00:09:25.990
and sometimes economics dictates that you'll
have to eat your butterscotch dilly bar

00:09:25.990 --> 00:09:27.560
next to the bail bondsman.

00:09:27.560 --> 00:09:30.820
But tolerating a little disharmony, a dash
of kitsch

00:09:30.820 --> 00:09:34.920
and the occasional strip club in a strip mall
along the Texas gulf coast

00:09:34.920 --> 00:09:39.460
is a small down payment on the right to be
the architect of your own life.

00:09:48.860 --> 00:09:52.519
Because if you're Jay Austin, you can build
the home of your dreams,

00:09:52.519 --> 00:09:54.610
you just can't live there.

00:09:54.610 --> 00:09:58.670
For now his tiny house is a part time residence
and a full time showpiece

00:09:58.670 --> 00:10:01.870
to present to the public in the hopes of changing
a zoning committe

00:10:01.870 --> 00:10:05.360
that hasn't updated a zoning code in 56 years.

